<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahakuta group of temples</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahakuta group of temples is a complex of Shaiva temples near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of Badami in the sixth and seventh centuries around a sacred spring-fed tank. Its principal shrine is the Mahakuteshwara temple. A pillar inscription of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalesha">Mangalesha</a> found there, dated around 602, is an important source for early Chalukya history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahakuta-group-of-temples.jpg" alt="Mahakuta group of temples" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi (talk) 03:16, 13 August 2008 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahakuta_group_of_temples1_at_Mahakuta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
